---
title: Proposition 3
---
order: 3
---
short_label: VI.3
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If an angle of a triangle is bisected by a straight line cutting the base, then the segments of the base have the same ratio as the remaining sides of the triangle; and, if segments of the base have the same ratio as the remaining sides of the triangle, then the straight line joining the vertex to the point of section bisects the angle of the triangle.
----
proof:

Let *ABC* be a triangle, and let the angle *BAC* be bisected by the straight line *AD.*

I say that *DB* is to *DC* as *AB* is to *AC.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="300" height="290" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.3 diagram" src="propVI3.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "VI.3",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;160,130",
            "B;point;free;40,250",
            "C;point;free;220,250",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            "AD;line;angleBisector;B,A,C",
            "D;point;last;AD",
            "E';point;parallelogram;A,D,C;0;0",
            "E;point;intersection;B,A,C,E'",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Draw *CE* through *C* parallel to *DA,* and carry *AB* through to meet it at *E.* [!just I.31]

Then, since the straight line *AC* falls upon the parallels *AD* and *EC,* the angle *ACE* equals the angle *CAD.* [!just I.29]

But the angle *CAD* equals the angle *BAD* by hypothesis, therefore the angle *BAD* also equals the angle *ACE.*

Again, since the straight line *BAE* falls upon the parallels *AD* and *EC,* the exterior angle *BAD* equals the interior angle *AEC.* [!just I.29]

But the angle *ACE* was also proved equal to the angle *BAD,* therefore the angle *ACE* also equals the angle *AEC,* so that the side *AE* also equals the side *AC.* [!just I.6]

And, since *AD* is parallel to *EC,* one of the sides of the triangle *BCE,* therefore, proportionally *DB* is to *DC* as *AB* is to *AE.* [!just VI.2]

But *AE* equals *AC,* therefore *DB* is to *DC* as *AB* is to *AC.* [!just V.7]

<hr width="50%">

Next, let *DB* be to *DC* as *AB* is to *AC.* Join *AD.*

I say that the straight line *AD* bisects the angle *BAC.*

With the same construction, since *DB* is to *DC* as *AB* is to *AC,* and also *DB* is to *DC* as *AB* is to *AE,* for *AD* is parallel to *EC,* one of the sides of the triangle *BCE,* therefore also *AB* is to *AC* as *AB* is to *AE.* [!just VI.2; V.11]

Therefore *AC* equals *AE,* so that the angle *AEC* also equals the angle *ACE.* [!just V.9; I.5]

But the angle *AEC* equals the exterior angle *BAD,* and the angle *ACE* equals the alternate angle *CAD,* therefore the angle *BAD* also equals the angle *CAD.* [!just I.29]

Therefore the straight line *AD* bisects the angle *BAC.*

Therefore, *if an angle of a triangle is bisected by a straight line cutting the base, then the segments of the base have the same ratio as the remaining sides of the triangle; and, if segments of the base have the same ratio as the remaining sides of the triangle, then the straight line joining the vertex to the point of section bisects the angle of the triangle.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition characterizes an angle bisector of an angle in a triangle as the line that partitions the base into parts proportional to the adjacent sides. The second part of the statement of the proposition is the converse of the first part of the statement. The proof relies on basic properties of triangles and parallel lines developed in Book I along with the result of the previous proposition @VI.2.

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is not used in the remainder of the *Elements.*
